---
id: org.dxos.app.composer.perf
name: Composer Performance
version: 0.1.0
---

Performance flows: journeys measured rather than asserted. Each `test` here is the top-level
description of a flow whose stages a Playwright spec drives and instruments — this document says
what the flow is and what "too slow" would mean, and
[`src/playwright/perf-projects.spec.ts`](../src/playwright/perf-projects.spec.ts) is the same flow
as executable stages. A step's `id` IS the stage id the harness records, so the description and the
numbers join without a second mapping to keep in sync.

Functional QA lives in [`APP.mdl`](./APP.mdl). Nothing here gates a merge: the flows run nightly
from `main` and trend into PostHog.

## Extensions

| Term    | URI                      |
| ------- | ------------------------ |
| `test`  | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`  |
| `step`  | `org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0`  |
| `suite` | `org.dxos.mdl.suite@1.0` |

The dialect is [`packages/reflect/deus/lang/qa.mdl`](../../../reflect/deus/lang/qa.mdl).

## Measurement contract

What the harness (`@dxos/perf-harness`) records per stage, and the rules that make two runs
comparable at all:

- **Two modes, never mixed.** `measure` reads counters at stage boundaries and runs the V8 sampling
  profiler, writing no profiles, so that per-realm CPU is trended; it is the sole mode trended.
  `diagnose` adds a screencast and keeps the profiles. The line between them is the SCREENCAST:
  measured on one flow, profiler-only costs +2.6% against `measure` — below the run-to-run noise —
  while adding the screencast costs +47%, because it encodes a JPEG per frame and waits on an ack
  before the next. Note also that Playwright's own Network domain makes Blink retain response
  bodies in EVERY mode (the finding behind `scripts/memory/plain-soak.mjs`), so the absolute
  footprint is inflated by an unmeasured constant while a trend across runs stays valid.
- **Per stage:** wall ms; total CPU ms across every Chrome process (`SystemInfo.getProcessInfo`);
  CPU per REALM from the profiler, the only instrument that reaches a worker — process CPU folds a
  dedicated worker into its renderer, and the `Performance` domain does not exist on a worker target
  at all; the page main thread's `script`/`layout`/`recalcStyle` split; JS heap per target after a
  forced GC; the renderers' private footprint, with Chrome's own processes reported separately;
  DOM node and listener counts; network bytes
  split code-load vs API; long tasks and TBT for the page, and timer-drift lag per realm.
- **Recorded on every row, or the trend is noise:** serving mode (`vite preview` over a production
  bundle — `vite serve` costs ~2.5x on the main thread), plugin set, profile state (first-run
  performs onboarding and loads a different module set), settle time, and which instruments were
  attached. Per `scripts/memory/README.md` §"Comparing runs".
- **Memory means four different things** that differ by 3-5x. The trended one is the renderers'
  private footprint: it counts wasm linear memory, where automerge documents live outside every
  JS-heap reading, and unlike RSS it is disjoint per process, so the renderers can be added. A peak
  of RSS summed over the whole process tree, which this replaced, multi-counted the shared pages
  every process maps and buried the app under ~218 MB of Chrome's own.

## QA

```mdl
test QA-1: A project with a large task set and long documents
  desc: |
    One journey across TWO different cost engines, because a project is not a task list and
    anything in the app can tank performance.

    The task set is the ECHO-shaped half: `TaskSet.tasks` is FLAT and holds every task in the set,
    sub-tasks included, so a large set is a large ref array: `TaskSet.loadRefs` resolves the cold
    remainder at concurrency 16, `resolveTasks` de-duplicates the whole array on every read, and the
    hierarchy the UI renders is derived by walking `Task.parentTask` per task — every one O(set
    size) on a structure the UI reads on each render.

    The documents are the editor-shaped half: markdown filed as project artifacts, where the cost is
    CodeMirror's line measurement, highlighting and the keystroke path rather than any ref array. A
    regression in one half would be invisible in the other, which is why one flow covers both.

    Measured, not asserted: the only failure is a stage that cannot complete.
  actors: agent
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space created by the fixture, named "PERF: Projects $runId"
    - >-
      scale: ONE size, `tasks=200,depth=2,projects=1,docs=3x400` — shaped like a project someone
      actually has. Deliberately not a set of tiers: data volume is orthogonal to the flow, and a
      scale knob makes every row's comparability depend on it. The label is recorded on each row so
      a trend breaks visibly if the fixture changes shape; someone exploring behaviour at size edits
      `SCALE` in `perf/fixture.ts` for that run
    - >-
      THE FIXTURE PATH IS WHY THE SIZE IS WHAT IT IS. Generation costs ~420ms per task through the
      operation layer (five samples: 80.8-84.6s for 200 tasks), because each task is one
      `tasks.create` whose write appends to a growing `tasks` array — so the rate gets WORSE as the
      array grows, not better. Two attempts at 2,000 tasks establish that: a flat 15-minute budget
      expired mid-fixture, and so did a 24-minute budget derived from the 200-task rate, neither
      reaching a single stage, which bounds 2,000 tasks above 1,380s (over 690ms each). Anything
      materially larger wants the archive path (`buildArchive` -> `client.spaces.import`, building
      the graph headlessly in node and importing it), which is unbuilt
    - the fixture has created `projects` Projects via `org.dxos.operation.projects.create`, each with
      `tasks / projects` tasks filed into its own task set via `org.dxos.operation.tasks.create`,
      nested to `depth` and spread across three milestones
    - >-
      the fixture has created `documents` markdown documents via
      `org.dxos.operation.markdown.create`, each `documentParagraphs` paragraphs long with a heading
      every tenth, and filed each onto a project via `org.dxos.operation.projects.addArtifact` —
      artifacts rather than loose objects, because that is how a project accumulates documents in
      use and it puts them on the Overview tab this flow already opens
    - >-
      paragraph text varies word by word rather than repeating one string: an editor's line
      measurement and highlight pass both behave differently on identical lines, so a document of
      one repeated paragraph would measure a cache rather than a render
    - fixture generation happens BEFORE the first measured stage and is never itself a stage —
      its cost is setup, not a number anyone reads
    - plugin-projects and plugin-tasks are enabled (both are on by default)
    - every generated task carries the creation default status `todo` — `tasks.create` takes no
      status, so a row component's other status branches are NOT exercised by this fixture; varying
      them needs a second `tasks.update` pass and would change what the numbers cover
  steps:
    - name: Boot to ready
      id: boot
      do: Open the app and wait for the user-account control.
      expect: the app reaches ready with no error logged
      note: |
        Cold profile, so this stage carries onboarding and the whole boot module graph. Comparable
        only against other `first-run` rows — which is why `profileState` is on every row.
    - name: Wait for the space to replicate
      id: await-replication
      do: Poll the space's sync state until it has caught up with EDGE.
      expect: nothing outstanding, or 95% replicated held for ten seconds
      note: |
        The stage every stage below it depends on. The fixture's writes keep replicating long after
        the last `tasks.create` resolves, so without this wait whichever stage happened to be
        running absorbed setup's SQLite writes and socket traffic — which is why the per-stage disk
        and network columns varied far more run to run than the stages themselves did.

        Read from `space.db.getSyncState()`, the combined automerge-documents and feed-blocks view
        already selected against the EDGE peer, with `space.db.getAutomergeSyncState()` read beside
        it for the peer LIST: the combined view reports zeroes for every document field when no
        EDGE peer is present, so "replicated" and "not replicating" are otherwise the same reading.
        `progress` is `1 - (unsynced documents + blocks to push + blocks to pull) / (documents known
        to either peer + blocks held locally)`.

        Two ways to finish, because a strict zero is not reachable: nothing outstanding, or 95% held
        for ten seconds. The app keeps producing small mutations of its own, so the last few
        documents can stay outstanding indefinitely while the fixture is long since on the remote —
        waiting for a true zero would trade a noisy measurement for a timeout. Dropping back under
        95% restarts the ten seconds.

        Its own columns are setup's cost, not the app's: read it as the absorber that makes the
        stages after it clean, rather than as a measurement.

        Budget is `DX_PERF_REPLICATION_TIMEOUT_MS`, 180s by default. A timeout FAILS the stage,
        logging every reading where something changed, so the row is never trended and the run says
        the stages after it carried setup's replication rather than presenting them as measurements.
    - name: Open the fixture space
      id: open-space
      do: Pick the "PERF: Projects" space from the space list.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.switchWorkspace { subject: "root/$given.space.id" }
      expect: the workspace is the fixture space and its navtree lists the projects
    - name: Open the project
      id: open-project
      do: Click the first project in the navtree.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.open { subject: ["root/$given.space.id/content/$given.project.id"] }
      expect: ProjectArticle renders on the Overview tab, showing the task-set summary and artifacts
      note: |
        Overview derives per-task-set open/total counts, so this stage pays one full pass over the
        set before a single task row is rendered.
    - name: Render the task list
      id: open-tasks
      do: Click the Tasks tab.
      expect: the hierarchical task list renders and the first rows are visible
      note: |
        The stage this flow exists for. `domNodes` is the diagnostic to read beside `wallMs`: a list
        whose node count rises with the set rather than the viewport is not virtualized, and that is
        a different fix from a slow query.
    - name: Toggle a task
      id: toggle-task
      do: Check the first visible task's checkbox.
      expect: the row shows as done and the change persists
      note: |
        One small write against a large set — the stage that would expose a write path that
        re-resolves the whole `tasks` array to update one member, and the O(set) read on the
        INTERACTION path rather than the open path.

        There is deliberately no filter stage. `TaskSetArticle` renders its toolbar (and with it the
        `tasks.filter` field) only as a standalone article; embedded as a Section, which is how
        `ProjectArticle` hosts it, it renders the bare list because the host owns the chrome. A
        filter stage would have to open the task set on its own surface, which is a different
        journey from this one.
    - name: Scroll the task list
      id: scroll-tasks
      do: Scroll the list to the end and back.
      expect: the list scrolls without a blank viewport
      note: |
        Read `stillFrameMaxMs` here (diagnose mode): scroll is the one stage where a frame gap is
        precisely the symptom a user reports.
    - name: Open a document artifact
      id: open-document
      do: Switch to the Overview tab and click the first document in the artifacts gallery.
      expect: the markdown editor renders the document
      note: |
        The artifact gallery renders `ObjectCard`s, which carry no testid of their own, so the card
        is addressed by title within the `projectsPlugin.artifacts` fieldset. This stage is a
        different engine from every stage before it: nothing here touches the `tasks` array.
    - name: Scroll the document
      id: scroll-document
      do: Scroll the editor to the end and back.
      expect: the document scrolls without a blank viewport
      note: |
        CodeMirror measures lines lazily, so this is where a long document's measurement cost lands
        rather than at open. Read `stillFrameMaxMs` beside `wallMs`, as with `scroll-tasks`.
    - name: Edit the document
      id: edit-document
      do: Type a short sentence into the editor.
      expect: the typed text appears in the document
      note: |
        Typed with a delay rather than set programmatically: the keystroke path — input handling,
        the CRDT write, re-highlight — is what a user feels in a long document, and a programmatic
        set would skip all of it. The stage to watch for a per-keystroke O(document) cost.
    - name: Reopen the project
      id: reopen-project
      do: Reopen the project from the navtree and return to the Tasks tab.
      expect: the task list renders again
      note: |
        Last deliberately: by now the journey has genuinely navigated away, into the editor. Catches
        cost paid per mount rather than once — a warm re-render should be far cheaper than
        `open-tasks`, and a run where it is not has cached nothing.
    - name: Open the assistant
      id: open-assistant
      do: Open the project's assistant companion.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.appToolkit.updateCompanion { subject: "root/$given.space.id/ai/org.dxos.type.project/$given.project.id/~assistant-chat" }
      expect: the companion chat renders with an editable prompt
      note: |
        Opening the companion provisions the project's chat, so this stage pays chat creation and
        skill binding as well as the chat surface's first render.
    - name: Run a scripted agent loop
      id: assistant-turns
      do: Send a prompt and wait for the assistant's closing answer.
      expect: twenty database-query tool calls, each preceded by thinking and a status line, then a final answer
      note: |
        Booted with `?model=scripted`, which replaces every assistant language model with the offline
        script in `src/testing/scripted-model.ts`: each turn emits a reasoning block, a `<status>`
        line and one `space-query-objects` call, rotating across query shapes, and the twenty-first
        answers. A live model's latency and variable tool use would otherwise be most of the number;
        this measures the app's side of an agent loop — streaming render, tool dispatch, the query
        itself — with a fixed 250 ms per turn that the wall time includes.
  after:
    - name: Delete the fixture space
      do: Delete the space from its settings.
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.delete { space: Space($given.space.id) }
      expect: no "PERF:" space remains
```

## Suites

```mdl
suite perf-nightly: The measured flows, from main
  desc: |
    Runs both modes of every flow. `measure` rows go to PostHog; `diagnose` artifacts are uploaded
    for whoever reads a regression and never trended.
  tags: [perf, nightly]
  tests: [QA-1]
```
